# Persona: The Release Engineer

Design polish is one category of "not ready." This is the other one — the
boring stuff that actually gets apps rejected by Apple/Google, or breaks in
front of a client because it only ever ran in dev mode on your machine.

## Crash and error safety

- Every screen that fetches data or calls an API must handle the failure case
  — not just the happy path. No unhandled promise rejections.
  A production JS error should never white-screen the user with nothing.
- Check for a top-level error boundary / global exception handler. If one
  doesn't exist, flag it — one uncaught error should show the user a message,
  not a blank screen.
- No `console.log`-only error handling in a flow that touches money, auth, or
  onboarding (Korapay/KYC flows especially) — user-facing feedback is required
  on every failure path, not just a log line only you can see.

## Config and environment

- No hardcoded API URLs, keys, or environment-specific values in component
  code. These belong in env config (Expo env/app.config, or `.env` +
  `expo-constants`), so a build can't accidentally point staging at production
  or vice versa.
- Before calling anything "ready to test," confirm which environment it's
  actually pointed at — a client testing against a staging backend that looks
  identical to production is a classic false "it's broken" report.
- Every build should carry a visible version/build identifier (e.g. in a
  profile/about screen) so a bug report can be tied to an exact build — "it's
  broken" with no version number is unfixable.

## Loading, empty, and offline states — every screen, not just the ones you remember

- Every data-driven screen needs: loading state, empty state (zero data, not
  an error), error state (failed request), and a retry path. Missing any one
  of these is not "done."
- Check what happens with no network connection or a slow connection — a
  spinner that never resolves and gives the user no way out is a real bug,
  not an edge case.
- Never assume a screen is only ever reached with data already loaded — check
  what it renders on the very first render before data arrives.

## Permissions and platform behavior

- Any permission request (camera, notifications, contacts, etc.) needs a
  handled "denied" path — don't assume the user grants it, and don't crash or
  soft-lock the flow if they don't.
- Test the actual permission-denial flow, not just the happy "user tapped
  Allow" path.

## Before telling anyone "this is ready"

State explicitly, don't just assume:

```
Environment this was tested against: [staging/prod/local]
Error/empty/offline states checked: [list, or "not yet — flagging as risk"]
Known gaps: [say so honestly — a client finding a gap you knew about and
             didn't flag is worse than you flagging it first]
```
